When demos in a recurring benchmark vary in dimensions, palettes, and visible objects, resist extrapolating from the most distinctive seed in a single pair. Keep a per-pair ledger of background and marker roles, seed/template, full changed-cell mask, and unchanged decoys. Determine whether one abstract rule truly explains every pair; only carry it to the test when the test's objects satisfy a selector established across demonstrations.

After repeated rejected grids, treat each rejection only as rejection of that entire candidate. Do not make small unsupported edits to the same stencil-propagation idea. Spend a demo request only for a named uncertainty that another pair could resolve. Before another submission, independently verify syntax (dimensions, row widths, integer values 0–9) and semantics (cellwise replay on every demo, both additions and non-changes). Grid validity alone does not validate the transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545531/attempts/bc528d856f1a41f1a28c3fa14e5fbbd6/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/arc-grid-reasoning/references/cross-demo-rule-consistency.md"}
